[82.102839, "o", "  qwen3.8-flash-next — 18 tool call(s) so far\r\n  ⚙ note \r\n    ✓ note — pip broke in /app container because site-packages/pip/ was d\r\nFixed — pip works again.\r\n\r\n## What was actually wrong\r\n\r\nYour Python install wasn't corrupted; it had a **half-finished uninstall**. The `pip` package directory was gone from `site-packages`, but its metadata folder was still there:\r\n\r\n```\r\nsite-packages/\r\n  README.txt\r\n  pip-24.3.1.dist-info/     <- metadata says \"pip 24.3.1 is installed\"\r\n  (pip/ missing)            <- the actual code is gone\r\n```\r\n\r\n`RECORD` in that dist-info listed 852 files including `pip/__init__.py`, none of which existed. So the launcher scripts at `/usr/local/bin/pip` still ran and tried `from pip._internal.cli.main import main` → `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'`.\r\n\r\n## The trap worth knowing about\r\n\r\nThe obvious repair, `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade`, **silently did nothing** — it printed `Requirement already satisfied: pip` and returned success. It reads that stale dist-in"]
[82.103031, "o", "fo and concludes pip is present. That no-op is what makes this state sticky; running it repeatedly looks like it's failing to help.\r\n\r\nI had to move the phantom dist-info aside first (backed up to `/tmp/pip-repair-backup/`, not deleted), after which ensurepip installed normally.\r\n\r\n## Repair path\r\n\r\nNo network was needed. The bundled wheel at `ensurepip/_bundled/pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl` matched the missing version exactly, and I verified it before trusting it — all 436 RECORD sha256 hashes checked, `zipfile.testzip()` clean. Then:\r\n\r\n```\r\nmv .../pip-24.3.1.dist-info /tmp/pip-repair-backup/\r\npython3 -m ensurepip --upgrade --default-pip\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Verification\r\n\r\n`pip --version`, `python3 -m pip --version`, all three shims (`pip`, `pip3`, `pip3.13`), and `import pip` work. A real round-trip passed: downloaded `six`, installed it, imported it, then uninstalled it. `pip check` reports no broken requirements. Site-packages is back to a clean `pip` + `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` pair, and my test package is removed"]
[82.103086, "o", ".\r\n\r\nTwo things to note: pip 24.3.1 is quite old (26.3 is current) — `pip install --upgrade pip` will now work, since pip is what was broken. And you're installing as root into the system interpreter; the warning pip prints about using a virtualenv is legitimate advice, as root-level installs into `/usr/local` are how you got into this state in the first place.\r\n\r\n[result] role=implementor model=qwen3.8-flash-next tools=18 surface=b1d6c95da52f\r\n"]
